Interoperability Notice
-----------------------

libane interoperates with Apple Neural Engine hardware through macOS public
programming interfaces (the Objective-C runtime, `dlopen`, `objc_msgSend`)
and by observing file-format outputs produced by macOS's own compiler
toolchain.

libane does not:
  - Redistribute Apple binaries, frameworks, or source code.
  - Disassemble or decompile Apple code.
  - Circumvent any technological protection measure or access control.
  - Process encrypted models; the `isEncryptedModel:NO` code path is the
    only one invoked.

Access to private Apple classes in libane is performed through documented
Objective-C runtime APIs (`objc_copyClassList`, `objc_copyMethodList`,
`class_copyMethodList`, `NSClassFromString`, `sel_registerName`,
`objc_msgSend`) and documented POSIX APIs (`dlopen`).

Some components (specifically HWX binary format analysis for cross-op
patching) involve observing the file-format outputs of Apple's compiler
to identify structural offsets. To the extent such activity constitutes
reverse engineering, it is undertaken solely for the purpose of achieving
interoperability between an independently-created computer program
(libane) and other programs (the Apple Neural Engine runtime), pursuant
to:

  - 17 U.S.C. § 1201(f) (reverse engineering exemption for
    interoperability), and
  - The fair-use doctrine as applied to intermediate copying for
    interoperability purposes in Sega Enterprises Ltd. v. Accolade, Inc.,
    977 F.2d 1510 (9th Cir. 1992), and Sony Computer Entertainment, Inc.
    v. Connectix Corp., 203 F.3d 596 (9th Cir. 2000).

No information obtained in the course of achieving interoperability is
used for any purpose other than enabling libane to interoperate with the
Apple Neural Engine, nor is any such information disclosed except where
necessary to describe the interoperability achieved (e.g., in source-code
comments, test cases, and research notes included in this repository).
